    "\n",
    "**Background: Week 2 Day 1 lecture recap:**\n",
    "\n",
    "Machine Learning is a field of AI where computers learn patterns from data to make predictions without being explicitly programmed for every rule. Before any model can be trained, the data must be prepared carefully.\n",
    "\n",
    "> - **Types of ML:** *Supervised* (labelled data → learn input–output mapping), *Unsupervised* (no labels → find hidden patterns), *Reinforcement* (agent learns via rewards/penalties).\n",
    "> - **ML Workflow:** Problem Definition → Data Collection → Preprocessing → Feature Selection → Data Splitting → Model Training → Evaluation → Deployment.\n",
    "> - **Missing values:** zeros in clinical columns are impossible (e.g. Glucose=0) and must be treated as NaN before imputation.\n",
    "> - **Noise vs Outliers:** noise = small random errors affecting many points; outliers = rare extreme values affecting a few points strongly.\n",
    "> - **Feature Engineering:** creates new variables (ratios, interactions, bins) that better capture the underlying pattern.\n",
    "> - **Feature Scaling:** standardisation (z-score) and normalisation (min–max) bring features to the same scale, preventing large-magnitude features from dominating the model.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
